Under this assumption, each count kim\begin{document}$k_{i}^{m}$\end{document} can be modeled as a binomial realization of the original number of bacteria ni with dilution factor ϕm, as described in Eq. (1). Thus, the likelihood of observing kim\begin{document}$k_{i}^{m}$\end{document} is written as:

(10)

p(kim∣ni,Φ)=(nikim)(1ϕm)kim(1−1ϕm)ni−kim.
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} p\left(k_{i}^{m} \mid n_{i}, \Phi\right)=\binom{n_{i}}{k_{i}^{m}}\left(\frac{1}{\phi^{m}}\right)^{k_{i}^{m}}\left(1-\frac{1}{\phi^{m}}\right)^{n_{i}-k_{i}^{m}} . \tag{10} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



As previously described, the datapoint (ki, mi) corresponds to the counts and the index of the first value within the sequence (ki1,ki2,…,kiM)\begin{document}$\left(k_{i}^{1}, k_{i}^{2}, \ldots, k_{i}^{M}\right)$\end{document} that satisfies the condition kim≤kCO\begin{document}$k_{i}^{m} \leq k_{\mathrm{CO}}$\end{document}, where kCO is the cutoff. To formalize this, we define mi as the (superscript) index of the first plate where the counts fall within the cutoff:

(11)

mi=min{m∣kim≤kCO},
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} m_{i}=\min \left\{m \mid k_{i}^{m} \leq k_{\mathrm{CO}}\right\}, \tag{11} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



from which the observed datapoint (ki, ϕi) is obtained with:

(12)

ki=kimiandϕi=ϕmi.
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} k_{i}=k_{i}^{m_{i}} \quad \text { and } \quad \phi_{i}=\phi^{m_{i}} . \tag{12} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



To avoid confusion, hereinafter we express the likelihood in terms of the plate index mi rather than the corresponding dilution factor ϕi, since the ordered dilution schedule Φ establishes a one-to-one correspondence between them.

Within this convention. the likelihood, p(ki, mi | ni, Φ), is written as
(13)
p(ki,mi∣ni,Φ)=p(ki∣mi,ni,Φ)p(mi∣ni,Φ). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} p\left(k_{i}, m_{i} \mid n_{i}, \Phi\right)=p\left(k_{i} \mid m_{i}, n_{i}, \Phi\right) p\left(m_{i} \mid n_{i}, \Phi\right) . \tag{13} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Once more, we proceed by showing how to calculate each factor within the product above. Starting from the last factor, p(mi | ni, Φ), we note that if the plate with index mi is reported, we must have that both m<mi\begin{document}$m<m_{i}$\end{document},
(14)
p(mi∣ni,Φ)=P({kimi≤kCO}∣ni,Φ)∏m=1mi−1P({kim>kCO}∣ni,Φ). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} p\left(m_{i} \mid n_{i}, \Phi\right)=P\left(\left\{k_{i}^{m_{i}} \leq k_{\mathrm{CO}}\right\} \mid n_{i}, \Phi\right) \prod_{m=1}^{m_{i}-1} P\left(\left\{k_{i}^{m}>k_{\mathrm{CO}}\right\} \mid n_{i}, \Phi\right) . \tag{14} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Here, we use the capitalized P to represent that we are calculating the probability that a variable is within a set, rather than the probability of a single possible value. In this specific model, what we are asking is the probability that kim\begin{document}$k_{i}^{m}$\end{document} is smaller than or equal to kCO. This is given by summing the probability of every value of kim\begin{document}$k_{i}^{m}$\end{document} up to kCO, meaning
(15)
P({kim≤kCO}∣ni,Φ)=∑kim=0kCOp(kim∣ni,Φ), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} P\left(\left\{k_{i}^{m} \leq k_{\mathrm{CO}}\right\} \mid n_{i}, \Phi\right)=\sum_{k_{i}^{m}=0}^{k_{\mathrm{CO}}} p\left(k_{i}^{m} \mid n_{i}, \Phi\right), \tag{15} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
so (14) becomes
(16)
p(mi∣ni,Φ)=(∑kimi=0kCOp(kimi∣ni,Φ))[∏m=1mi−1(1−∑kim=0kCOp(kim∣ni,Φ))] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} p\left(m_{i} \mid n_{i}, \Phi\right)=\left(\sum_{k_{i}^{m_{i}}=0}^{k_{\mathrm{CO}}} p\left(k_{i}^{m_{i}} \mid n_{i}, \Phi\right)\right)\left[\prod_{m=1}^{m_{i}-1}\left(1-\sum_{k_{i}^{m}=0}^{k_{\mathrm{CO}}} p\left(k_{i}^{m} \mid n_{i}, \Phi\right)\right)\right] \tag{16} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
with each p(kim∣ni,Φ)\begin{document}$p\left(k_{i}^{m} \mid n_{i}, \Phi\right)$\end{document} given by (10).

With the prior for ρ\begin{document}$\rho$\end{document} established, we proceed to define the prior for the means μ\begin{document}$\mu$\end{document} and standard deviations σ¯\begin{document}$\bar{\sigma}$\end{document} . Here, each component α is independent from each other. Initially, for the means, we select a distribution that avoids biasing the choice by being nearly uniform, but preventing too small numbers and adhering to a sensible cutoff in n. The mean of each Gaussian must be smaller than twice the largest number found by naively multiplying counts per dilution; we denote this largest cutoff as N∗=2maxiϕiki\begin{document}$N^{*}=2 \max _{i} \phi_{i} k_{i}$\end{document}. Thus, the prior for each mean, µα, is given by
(20)
μαN∗∼Beta(1.1,1.1),p(μα)∝1N∗(μαN∗)0.1(1−μαN∗)0.1. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} \frac{\mu_{\alpha}}{N^{*}} & \sim \operatorname{Beta}(1.1,1.1), \\ p\left(\mu_{\alpha}\right) & \propto \frac{1}{N^{*}}\left(\frac{\mu_{\alpha}}{N^{*}}\right)^{0.1}\left(1-\frac{\mu_{\alpha}}{N^{*}}\right)^{0.1} . \tag{20} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
For the prior in σ¯\begin{document}$\bar{\sigma}$\end{document}, we write a prior that scales with its respective means, but is still broad; we choose a lognormal prior here
(21)
σα∣μα∼LogNormal(log⁡μα,1),p(σα∣μα)=12π1σαexp⁡[−(log⁡σα/μα)22]. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} \sigma_{\alpha} \mid \mu_{\alpha} & \sim \text { LogNormal }\left(\log \mu_{\alpha}, 1\right), \\ p\left(\sigma_{\alpha} \mid \mu_{\alpha}\right) & =\frac{1}{\sqrt{2 \pi}} \frac{1}{\sigma_{\alpha}} \exp \left[-\frac{\left(\log \sigma_{\alpha} / \mu_{\alpha}\right)^{2}}{2}\right] . \tag{21} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
Thus, the prior used to generate the figures in the main text is defined as:
(22)
p(θ)=p(ρ¯)∏α=1Ap(μα)p(σα∣μα), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} p(\theta)=p(\bar{\rho}) \prod_{\alpha=1}^{A} p\left(\mu_{\alpha}\right) p\left(\sigma_{\alpha} \mid \mu_{\alpha}\right), \tag{22} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
with p(ρ¯),p(μα)\begin{document}$p(\bar{\rho}), p\left(\mu_{\alpha}\right)$\end{document}, and p(σα|μα) given by (19), (20), and (21) respectively.
